It gives you a way to stop getting trapped by somebody else’s binary.

Practically, you can use it to do three things:

* Interrogate labels. When someone says “good,” “bad,” “right,” “wrong,” “loyal,” “selfish,” “weak,” “strong,” ask what variables they compressed into that label.
* Find the hidden degrees of freedom. Separate intent, action, context, consequence, timing, uncertainty, and who bears the cost. A judgment that looked absolute often turns out to depend on one or two hidden parameters.
* Create better moves. Once the frame is decomposed, you can look for an option that was invisible inside the original binary—neither submit nor rebel, neither attack nor retreat, neither accept nor deny.

So the useful operator is:

\text{binary judgment}
\rightarrow
\text{decompose dimensions}
\rightarrow
\text{find hidden variables}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruct choice space}

That is the payoff for you: more maneuver room.

Instead of arguing inside “Is this good or evil?”, you can ask, “What exactly is being judged, by what rule, under what conditions, and what changes if I alter one variable?”

That turns morality, narrative, social pressure, even self-judgment from a wall into an obstacle course.